2026-11-03T20:30:51.1628667Z    image gate: elbridge_keith_house_1900_prairie glessner_oblique: verdict "differs", 7 difference(s) open: the window over the porch is a PAIRED, arched window (two round lights under one wider head, about 1.25 m across; the c. 2014 detail and the 2024 front show it plainly); since T-3208 it is round-headed like its neighbours but still ONE light of the front's width, because a front laid by front_bays glazes every column with one K06 variant and K01's round-hooded light is a single sash; the south flank's front part is the same light STONE as the front in the print (the mid-century oblique, a print of the same negative, agrees; no later picture shows the flank); the build lays it in K03 brick, because the 1911 sheet records 'brick with stone front' and K01 returns stone along a flank only as a quoined return of at most 4 m, where the print shows about 6 m of flank and no quoins. Its windows there are square-headed, where the print's are round-headed under hoods; the bay's crown carries a DORMER on its front face (the print's x 600-680 px; the mid-century and 2024 fronts and the c. 2014 detail show it as an arched light under its own cornice); K08's mansard crown takes none, and nothing in the kit sets a dormer on a bay's roof; the porch's columns are a pair a side with responds and the balcony's rail stands about 0.5 m higher than built (0.35 m, the most K01's hood leaves under the window's sill) — T-3208; the terrace stands on carved brackets over the basement with its basement lights between them, and a low rail on it; built as a solid stone terrace — T-3208; the stoop's two square block newels at its foot, about 1.2 m high with carved caps (the print's x 600 and 700; the mid-century and 2024 fronts), and its iron hand-rails: the build's stoop has solid stone cheeks and no newels. K11's coping wall stands one stair pier on one side only and K07's balustrade would add balusters no picture shows; the corona over the bay reads about 1 m higher in the print than in the render at the bay's south cheek (y 181 against about 240 px), where the camera's fit and the 1911 sheet's bay disagree (the sheet bulges 1.8 m at v 2.0-6.1, the print 0.93 m at v 0.3-4.3). Weighed against the mid-century and 2024 fronts: in both the bay's cornice runs level with the front's (the brackets and the corona continue round the bay unbroken), which is how it is built, so the difference is the camera's fit at the bay's corner rather than the cornice; it stays open until a camera solved on a front confirms it, since a re-fit is not this sign-off's
2026-11-03T20:30:51.1641995Z    image gate: first_fort_dearborn_pickets_outer currey_1912_birdseye: verdict "differs", 4 difference(s) open: Blockhouses (4, 5): the drawing tops each with a small square cupola under its own pyramid cap, above a jettied upper storey; ours stop at a plain pyramid roof. Whistler's 1808 index says 'the Cupolas are not yet built on the Block Houses as laid down', so his plan drew them and they were still to come in February 1808; whether they stood by 1812 is unrecorded. Owner, 2026-11-03 (T-3335): adopt it; the fix belongs to T-3041.; Western gateway (3): the drawing opens a gate in the west side beside the north-west blockhouse; ours has none. Quaife (1913) also puts a wicket gate 'near the northwestern blockhouse' (Whistler index no. 8), but the register could not find it on the 1808 sheet, so it was not built. Owner, 2026-11-03 (T-3335): adopt it; the fix belongs to T-3041.; Magazine (8): the drawing gives it a rounded, barrel-vaulted roof at the west end of the north side; ours stands in the same place with a gable roof. Whistler's elevation draws an arched front, which the record already notes 'may be a vault'. Owner, 2026-11-03 (T-3335): adopt it; the fix belongs to T-3041.; North-east inner angle: the drawing has a small square building with a pyramid roof there; ours has Whistler's small house (index no. 16) in the same angle with a gable roof. Owner, 2026-11-03 (T-3335): adopt it; the fix belongs to T-3041.
